From: WL2GO
Subject: Reset the ARQ timeout when our IDLE keepalive is answered

The ISS with nothing to send goes to protocol state IDLE and transmits an
IDLE frame every ~3 seconds; the IRS answers each one with a DataACK. That
exchange is the protocol's keepalive -- and upstream ages it asymmetrically.
The IRS restarts its timeout clock on every IDLE it receives
(ProcessRcvdARQFrame, IRS case, IDLEFRAME handler: dttTimeoutTrip = Now,
both branches). The ISS's IDLE-state DataACK handler restarts nothing: it
logs "Ignore, will repeat IDLE" and returns, so the ISS counts ARQTimeout
(default 120 s) from the last actual DATA transfer no matter how many valid
ACKs arrive.

On a healthy link this means the ISS unilaterally declares the session dead
at exactly ARQTimeout while the IRS would have held it forever. Seen three
times in one afternoon against RMS Trimode gateway N0GWY: a message proposal
delivered and ACKed, the gateway's CMS backhaul slow to produce the FS
response, its modem ACKing our every keepalive with distance-0.01 decodes --
and our side, not the silent one, tearing the session down at 120 s.

The fix is one line: a valid in-session DataACK received in state IDLE
restarts dttTimeoutTrip, mirroring what the IRS already does for the IDLE
that provoked it. A session now survives for as long as the two modems can
actually hear each other; a genuinely dead link still times out exactly as
before, because a dead link produces no ACKs. Runaway sessions remain
bounded by the host (Pat / the operator's abort) and by the far gateway's
own session limits.

diff --git a/src/common/ARQ.c b/src/common/ARQ.c
index 1b44fa7..57c450b 100644
--- a/src/common/ARQ.c
+++ b/src/common/ARQ.c
@@ -1766,6 +1766,17 @@ void ProcessRcvdARQFrame(UCHAR intFrameType, UCHAR * bytData, int DataLen, BOOL
 			return;  // No decode so continue to wait
 
 		if (intFrameType >= DataACKmin) {
+			// A valid DataACK carrying this session's ID is the IRS answering
+			// our IDLE keepalive: proof the link is alive in both directions.
+			// Restart the timeout clock, exactly as the IRS restarts its own
+			// on receiving that IDLE (see the IDLEFRAME handler in the IRS
+			// case above).  Without this the two sides age asymmetrically --
+			// the IRS holds the session for as long as IDLEs keep arriving,
+			// while the ISS counts ARQTimeout from the last DATA transfer and
+			// unilaterally disconnects a perfectly healthy link.  Seen in the
+			// field as a Winlink gateway with a slow CMS backhaul ACKing every
+			// keepalive for two minutes while we, not it, gave up.
+			dttTimeoutTrip = Now;
 			if (bytDataToSendLength > 0)  // If ACK and Data to send
 			{
 				ZF_LOGI("[ARDOPprotocol.ProcessedRcvdARQFrame] Protocol state IDLE, ACK Received with Data to send. Go to ISS Data state.");
